### What is the alternate printing problem?

The alternate printing problem asks you to use two threads to produce interleaved output. Thread-1 prints numbers (1, 2, 3, ...) and Thread-2 prints letters (A, B, C, ...). The output must be strictly alternating: 1, A, 2, B, 3, C. This is a classic interview question testing understanding of inter-thread communication using wait() and notify().

### How do you solve it with wait/notify?

The solution uses a shared boolean flag to control turns. Both threads synchronize on the same lock object, check the flag in a while loop, and call wait() when it is not their turn. After printing, the active thread flips the flag and calls notify() to wake the other thread.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

public class AlternatePrinter {
    private boolean numberTurn = true;
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    public void printNumbers(int count) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= count; i++) {
            synchronized (lock) {
                while (!numberTurn) {
                    try { lock.wait(); } catch (InterruptedException e) { return; }
                }
                System.out.print(i + ",");
                numberTurn = false;
                lock.notify();
            }
        }
    }

    public void printLetters(int count) {
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
            synchronized (lock) {
                while (numberTurn) {
                    try { lock.wait(); } catch (InterruptedException e) { return; }
                }
                System.out.print((char) ('A' + i) + ",");
                numberTurn = true;
                lock.notify();
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AlternatePrinter printer = new AlternatePrinter();
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> printer.printNumbers(5), "NumberThread");
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> printer.printLetters(5), "LetterThread");
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}
```

