### Why must you use while instead of if for the wait condition?

A thread can wake up without being notified, which is called a spurious wakeup. The JVM specification allows this behavior. If you use `if (!numberTurn) wait()`, the thread may resume even though the flag has not changed, print out of order, and break the alternation. The `while` loop re-checks the condition after every wakeup, ensuring the thread only proceeds when it is genuinely its turn.

```java
// WRONG: if allows spurious wakeup to break alternation
synchronized (lock) {
    if (!numberTurn) {
        lock.wait();  // spurious wakeup: proceeds even if flag unchanged!
    }
    System.out.print(i);  // may print out of order
}

// CORRECT: while re-checks after every wakeup
synchronized (lock) {
    while (!numberTurn) {
        lock.wait();  // spurious wakeup: loops back and checks again
    }
    System.out.print(i);  // only proceeds when it is genuinely our turn
}
```

### How do you solve it with Lock and Condition?

ReentrantLock with two Condition objects provides a cleaner separation. Each thread waits on its own Condition and signals the other thread's Condition.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.locks.Condition;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class AlternatePrinterLock {
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private final Condition numberCondition = lock.newCondition();
    private final Condition letterCondition = lock.newCondition();
    private boolean numberTurn = true;

    public void printNumbers(int count) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= count; i++) {
            lock.lock();
            try {
                while (!numberTurn) numberCondition.await();
                System.out.print(i + ",");
                numberTurn = false;
                letterCondition.signal();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) { return;
            } finally { lock.unlock(); }
        }
    }

    public void printLetters(int count) {
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
            lock.lock();
            try {
                while (numberTurn) letterCondition.await();
                System.out.print((char) ('A' + i) + ",");
                numberTurn = true;
                numberCondition.signal();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) { return;
            } finally { lock.unlock(); }
        }
    }
}
```

### How do you generalize to N threads?

Replace the boolean flag with an int that cycles from 0 to N-1. Each thread checks if the current turn matches its index. Use `notifyAll()` instead of `notify()` because you need to wake all threads so the correct one can proceed.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

public class NThreadAlternate {
    private int currentTurn = 0;
    private final int totalThreads;
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    public NThreadAlternate(int totalThreads) {
        this.totalThreads = totalThreads;
    }

    public void print(int threadId, String[] values) {
        for (String value : values) {
            synchronized (lock) {
                while (currentTurn != threadId) {
                    try { lock.wait(); } catch (InterruptedException e) { return; }
                }
                System.out.print(value + ",");
                currentTurn = (currentTurn + 1) % totalThreads;
                lock.notifyAll();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### What are the common mistakes?

The most common mistake is calling notify() before wait(). If Thread-1 calls notify() but Thread-2 has not yet called wait(), the notification is lost and Thread-2 waits forever. The shared flag prevents this: Thread-2 checks the flag first and only calls wait() if it is not its turn.

```java
// WRONG: notify before wait is lost
Object lock = new Object();
// Thread-1 runs first:
synchronized (lock) { lock.notify(); }  // nobody waiting, signal LOST!
// Thread-2 runs second:
synchronized (lock) { lock.wait(); }    // waits FOREVER, nobody to notify

// CORRECT: flag prevents lost notification
boolean ready = false;
// Thread-1:
synchronized (lock) { ready = true; lock.notify(); }
// Thread-2:
synchronized (lock) { while (!ready) lock.wait(); }  // if ready is true, skips wait
```

### How is this applied in banking systems?

In a banking audit system, compliance requires that debit and credit log entries alternate strictly for reconciliation. A debit-logger thread and a credit-logger thread use this pattern to ensure the audit trail is interleaved correctly without any two consecutive debits or credits.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can you do this without wait/notify? Yes, using two Semaphores with initial permits of 1 and 0, or using LockSupport.park/unpark with a shared volatile flag.
- What happens if one thread is interrupted? The InterruptedException breaks the thread out of wait(), and the finally block should signal the other thread to prevent it from waiting forever.
- Can you use a BlockingQueue? Yes, two SynchronousQueues can act as handoff signals between the threads.
